<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Periyar River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Periyar is the longest river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>, in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, flowing about 244 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a>. A great flood in 1341 is said to have silted up the old port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muziris">Muziris</a> near its mouth and opened a new harbour at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Kochi</a>, which then grew into the region's main port. Its waters feed the Idukki and Mullaperiyar dams.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/periyar-river.jpg" alt="Periyar River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: KannanVM · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fish_net_over_Periyar_River_0.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Periyar is the longest river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> and has its largest discharge potential. It is one of the few perennial rivers in the region and gives drinking water to several major towns. It is about 244 kilometres long, with a catchment of 5,398 square kilometres, of which 5,284 are in Kerala and 114 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>. Its source is on Chokkampatti Mala in the remote forests of the Periyar Tiger Reserve in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a>. After some 48 kilometres it is joined by the Mullayar at Mullakudy. Near Aluva the river divides into two distributaries, one reaching the sea at Munambam and the other draining into the Vembanad backwaters at Varapuzha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Periyar has been called the Lifeline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>. It supplies irrigation, domestic water and a fishery, and about a quarter of the state's industries lie on its banks, mostly in the Eloor-Edayar region north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Kochi</a>. The Mullaperiyar dam was one of the earliest inter-basin transfers in peninsular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It met a demand for water but led to salt intrusion and pollution in the lower reaches. The Idukki Dam, commissioned in 1976, is the largest hydroelectric project in Kerala, a concrete double-curvature arch dam with generators of 780 MW at an underground station at Moolamattom, built by an India-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> joint venture. Diversions have left the Periyar with 22 per cent less of its average flow.</p><p>The control and safety of the Mullaperiyar dam and the fairness of its lease have been disputed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>. Kerala holds that the Periyar is an intrastate river, since it originates, flows and meets the sea entirely in Kerala. Tamil Nadu admits those facts but argues that it is inter-state because part of its catchment lies in Tamil Nadu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>South of the Mullaperiyar reservoir an unbroken stretch of about 350 square kilometres of unmodified rainforest lies within the Periyar Tiger Reserve. Of an estimated 3,800 species of flowering plants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>, 1,966 have been reported from the reserve. The Aluva Sivarathri festival is held every year on the sand bank at Aluva, and the town of Kalady is the birthplace of the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adi-shankara">Adi Shankara</a>, with a temple consecrated to him in 1910.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Periyar River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 14th century, after a flood of the <b>Periyar</b> opened a new harbour, the dynasty made Kochi its centre, and the port grew as older Muziris declined.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-cochin">Kingdom of Cochin</a></li></ul></section>
